    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Can You Go to Porto Santo by Boat From Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>30 November 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Yes, by ferry. The Lobo Marinho sails from Funchal to Porto Santo in about two and a half hours, usually leaving in the morning and coming back in the evening, and a flight takes about 25 minutes. Short private boat trips like ours stay on Madeira&#x27;s south coast and do not make the crossing.</p>

      <h2>How does the ferry to Porto Santo work?</h2><p>One ship, the Lobo Marinho, run by Porto Santo Line, carries passengers and cars between the two islands. Porto Santo lies about 71 km from Madeira, and the crossing usually takes about two and a half hours.</p><p>On a regular day the ferry leaves the port of Funchal at 08:00, arrives around 10:30 and sails back in the evening. Times change with the day of the week and the season, and extra sailings are added around holidays, so check the current timetable on the <a href="https://www.portosantoline.pt/en/" rel="noopener">Porto Santo Line website</a> before you plan anything.</p><p>Two things catch visitors out:</p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance.</strong> The ship stops for its yearly maintenance in winter. In 2026 that started in January. It was also out of service from 14 to 22 October 2026 for a repair, with no regular sailings that week.</li><li><strong>Weather.</strong> Sailings are cancelled or moved when the forecast is bad, and on a rough day the crossing can be hard on the stomach even on a big ship.</li></ul><h2>What is the crossing like?</h2><p>On a calm day, an easy ride. You leave Funchal with the city and the mountains behind you, spend a couple of hours on open water, and arrive as Porto Santo's low brown hills come up ahead, a very different shape from Madeira's peaks.</p><p>On a day with swell, the ship rolls, and two and a half hours is a long time to feel it. If you are unsure of your stomach:</p><ul><li>eat a light breakfast before the 08:00 sailing, not a large one;</li><li>sit low and near the middle of the ship, where the movement is smallest;</li><li>go out on deck for fresh air and look at the horizon, not at a screen.</li></ul><h2>Is flying quicker?</h2><p>Much quicker. Binter flies between Madeira and Porto Santo, and the flight takes about 25 minutes gate to gate. Even with the drive to Madeira's airport, east of Funchal, and the time at the airport, you save hours.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Ferry</th><th>Flight</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Time on the way</td><td>About 2 h 30</td><td>About 25 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Leaves from</td><td>The port of Funchal, in the city</td><td>Madeira Airport, Santa Cruz</td></tr><tr><td>Takes a car</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Weather risk</td><td>Swell cancels or moves sailings</td><td>Wind can delay or divert flights</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>A day trip from the city, travelling with a car</td><td>Short stays, people who get seasick</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why does a small private boat not cross to Porto Santo?</h2><p>Because the crossing is about 71 km of open Atlantic with nothing in between. A private trip on Madeira's south coast stays close to the shore, the marina and the shelter of the island. The way to Porto Santo is a different kind of sea: no cliffs to hide behind, and hours of exposed water each way.</p><p>Chifbay trips are built for the south coast. The <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip west to Fajã dos Padres and Ribeira Brava</a> runs 2h30 or 3h, and the Sunset Trip 2h or 2h30. Neither would get you to Porto Santo and back, and we would not try. If anyone offers you Porto Santo on a small boat as a casual day out, ask hard questions about the forecast, the boat and the licence.</p><h2>Is Porto Santo worth a day trip?</h2><p>Yes, if you want a beach day. Porto Santo is small, about 11 km long and 6 km wide, and most of its south coast is one beach of golden sand about 9 km long. That is the reason most people go: Madeira itself has very little natural golden sand, and Porto Santo has a lot of it.</p><p>With the ferry arriving around 10:30 and leaving in the evening, you get most of a day on the island. That is time to walk part of the beach, swim, eat in Vila Baleira, the small main town, and be back at the port well before sailing. If you want more than a beach day, stay a night and slow down.</p><h2>What can you do instead from Madeira itself?</h2><p>If the day is about the sea and you are short on time, stay on Madeira. A few ideas, each with its own kind of view:</p><ul><li><strong>See Porto Santo from the mountains.</strong> On clear days it is visible from Pico do Areeiro, at 1,818 metres. Go early, before the cloud builds.</li><li><strong>Walk to the eastern tip.</strong> <a href="/posts/ponta-de-sao-lourenco-guide">Ponta de São Lourenço</a> is a dry, bare peninsula that feels closer in spirit to Porto Santo than to the green middle of Madeira.</li><li><strong>Swim from a boat instead of a beach.</strong> The Day Trip stops twice to swim, at Fajã dos Padres and at Ribeira Brava, with paddle boards at the first stop.</li><li><strong>Look at the other islands.</strong> The Desertas, the long, empty islands on the horizon southeast of Funchal, are a nature reserve. Our <a href="/posts/desertas-islands-madeira-guide">Desertas Islands guide</a> explains how visits work.</li></ul><h2>How do you fit Porto Santo and a boat trip into one holiday?</h2><p>Put them on different days. The ferry leaves at 08:00 and comes back in the evening, which rules out both the Day Trip and the Sunset Trip on the same day.</p><p>Leave a spare day between them if you can. The same weather that moves a ferry sailing can also move a boat trip, and a day in hand lets you swap. Chifbay's free cancellation runs up to 24 hours before departure, and if the skipper calls off a trip because of the sea, you choose another date or a full refund.</p><p>A good order is the boat trip early in your holiday and Porto Santo later. If the sea cancels the boat, you still have days left to move it into.</p><p>If you are prone to seasickness, read our guide on <a href="/posts/seasickness-boat-trip-madeira-guide">seasickness on a boat trip in Madeira</a> before you choose the ferry over the plane. Then pick your sea day from <a href="/experiences">our two trips</a> on the south coast, where the water stays close to the cliffs.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How long is the ferry from Funchal to Porto Santo?</h3><p>About two and a half hours each way on the Lobo Marinho, run by Porto Santo Line.</p><h3>Can you visit Porto Santo as a day trip?</h3><p>Yes. On a regular day the ferry leaves Funchal at 08:00 and returns in the evening, which gives you most of a day on the island. Check the timetable for your date.</p><h3>Does Chifbay go to Porto Santo?</h3><p>No. Our trips stay on Madeira's south coast and leave from Marina do Funchal.</p><h3>Does the ferry run in winter?</h3><p>Mostly, but the ship stops for its yearly maintenance in winter. In 2026 that started in January, so check the dates before you plan.</p>

      <blockquote>Seventy-one kilometres of open sea is a ferry&#x27;s job. The cliffs west of Funchal are ours.</blockquote>
    </article>
